<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malwa Agency</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> Agency was a political agency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-india-agency">Central India Agency</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, through which a British political agent supervised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of western Malwa. It included states such as Ratlam, Jaora, Sitamau and Sailana, and smaller holdings such as Piploda. After independence its states joined Madhya Bharat in 1948 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Malwa Agency was formed in 1895 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> that had been under the authority of the British agent for Indore, together with those of the Western Malwa Agency, a sub-agency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-india-agency">Central India Agency</a> since 1854, which was abolished. The political agent had his headquarters at Neemuch (Nimach); the other chief towns of the region were Ratlam and Jaora.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The two Dewas States, Dewas Senior and Dewas Junior, were added to the agency in 1907. In 1925 it was joined with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhopawar-agency">Bhopawar Agency</a> to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and Bhopawar Agency, renamed the Malwa and Southern States Agency in 1927. The Dewas States passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhopal-agency">Bhopal Agency</a> in 1931, and in 1934 the agency was once again named the Malwa Agency. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> in 1947 the rulers of its states acceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a>, and the states were joined to the new Indian state of Madhya Bharat, which was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> on 1 November 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The salute states, in order of precedence, were Ratlam, whose ruler bore the title Maharaja Bahadur and a hereditary salute of 13 guns (15 locally); Jaora, under a Nawab with 13 guns; Sailana, under a Raja Bahadur with 11 guns; and Jhabua and Sitamau, each under a Raja with 11 guns.</p><p>The non-salute states were Piploda, whose ruler was styled Rao, originally Thakur, the Panth-Piploda Estate and Barkhera Panth State. The agency also covered portions of the territories of Gwalior State, Indore State, Tonk State and the two Dewas States, Senior and Junior.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malwa Agency” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jaora was a 13-gun salute state of the <b>Malwa Agency</b>, with a Muslim ruling house over a mainly Hindu population growing opium and cotton.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaora-state">Jaora State</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 20th century Piploda was separated from Jaora and placed directly under the <b>Malwa Agency</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/piploda-state">Piploda State</a></li><li><blockquote>Under British paramountcy it was a salute state of the <b>Malwa Agency</b>; Sajjan Singh reigned from 1893 to 1947.</blockquote><a href="/state/ratlam-state">Ratlam State</a></li><li><blockquote>The state paid tribute to the Scindias of Gwalior State and later came under British protection within the <b>Malwa Agency</b> of central India, with an 11-gun salute.</blockquote><a href="/state/sailana-state">Sailana State</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th century the state paid tribute to Sindhia, and after 1818 it came under British paramountcy as a salute state of the <b>Malwa Agency</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sitamau-state">Sitamau State</a></li></ul></section>
